The best approach for `File.ReadAllText`, `File.WriteAllText`, and `Directory.Exists` is to adopt the **`System.IO.Abstractions`** NuGet package — it's battle-tested and already provides interfaces and test helpers for all of these.

## Setup

**1. Install the package:**
```bash
dotnet add package System.IO.Abstractions
```

**2. Register in DI:**
```csharp
builder.Services.AddSingleton<IFileSystem, FileSystem>();
```

**3. Inject `IFileSystem` into your classes:**
```csharp
public class MyService(IFileSystem fileSystem)
{
    public string Read(string path) => fileSystem.File.ReadAllText(path);

    public void Write(string path, string content) => fileSystem.File.WriteAllText(path, content);

    public bool DirectoryExists(string path) => fileSystem.Directory.Exists(path);
}
```

## Testing

**Install the test helpers in your test project:**
```bash
dotnet add package System.IO.Abstractions.TestingHelpers
```

**Use `MockFileSystem` in tests:**
```csharp
var mockFs = new MockFileSystem(new Dictionary<string, MockFileData>
{
    { "/data/config.json", new MockFileData("{\"key\": \"value\"}") },
    { "/data/", new MockDirectoryData() }
});

var service = new MyService(mockFs);

Assert.True(service.DirectoryExists("/data"));
Assert.Equal("{\"key\": \"value\"}", service.Read("/data/config.json"));

service.Write("/data/out.txt", "hello");
Assert.Equal("hello", mockFs.File.ReadAllText("/data/out.txt"));
```

This covers all three calls (`File.ReadAllText`, `File.WriteAllText`, `Directory.Exists`) behind a single `IFileSystem` abstraction with no custom wrapper code to maintain.
